### How do you find the first non-repeating character?

Use an insertion-ordered count map with two passes. O(n) time, O(k) space.

```java
package com.bank.coding;

import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class FirstNonRepeating {
    public static Character find(String s) {
        if (s == null || s.isEmpty()) return null;
        Map<Character, Integer> counts = new LinkedHashMap<>();
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
            counts.merge(c, 1, Integer::sum);
        }
        for (Map.Entry<Character, Integer> entry : counts.entrySet()) {
            if (entry.getValue() == 1) return entry.getKey();
        }
        return null; // All characters repeat
    }
}
// Edge cases: empty string, all repeats, single character
```

### How do you reverse a string without built-ins?

Two-pointer swap or index loop from end. O(n) time, O(n) space.

```java
public static String reverse(String s) {
    if (s == null || s.length() <= 1) return s;
    char[] chars = s.toCharArray();
    int left = 0, right = chars.length - 1;
    while (left < right) {
        char temp = chars[left];
        chars[left] = chars[right];
        chars[right] = temp;
        left++;
        right--;
    }
    return new String(chars);
}
// Edge cases: empty, single char, Unicode surrogate pairs
```

### How do you remove duplicates from an array?

Add to a set for O(n). Use a seen-set to preserve order.

```java
public static List<Integer> dedupe(int[] arr) {
    Set<Integer> seen = new LinkedHashSet<>();
    for (int n : arr) seen.add(n);
    return new ArrayList<>(seen);
}
// Edge cases: empty array, all same values, already unique
```

### How do you deduplicate objects by a property?

Keep a set of seen property values, include an object only the first time its key appears.

```java
public static List<Account> dedupeByAccountNo(List<Account> accounts) {
    Set<String> seen = new HashSet<>();
    List<Account> result = new ArrayList<>();
    for (Account acc : accounts) {
        if (seen.add(acc.getAccountNo())) {
            result.add(acc);
        }
    }
    return result;
}
// Edge cases: null keys, empty list, all duplicates
```

### How do you find the second-highest salary?

Use DENSE_RANK or a MAX subquery.

```sql
-- DENSE_RANK approach (handles ties, scales to Nth)
SELECT salary FROM (
  SELECT salary, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rnk
  FROM employees
) ranked
WHERE rnk = 2;

-- Subquery approach (simpler for exactly second)
SELECT MAX(salary) AS second_highest
FROM employees
WHERE salary < (SELECT MAX(salary) FROM employees);

-- Both return NULL when only one distinct salary exists
```

### How do you handle ties and edge cases in SQL?

DENSE_RANK handles ties naturally. ROW_NUMBER does not.

```sql
-- DENSE_RANK: tied salaries share a rank
-- Salaries: 100000, 80000, 80000, 60000
-- Ranks:    1,       2,     2,     3
-- Second highest: 80000 (both rows)

-- ROW_NUMBER: each row gets a unique number
-- Salaries: 100000, 80000, 80000, 60000
-- Numbers:  1,       2,     3,     4
-- "Second row": only one of the 80000s

-- Edge case: single salary
SELECT MAX(salary) FROM employees
WHERE salary < (SELECT MAX(salary) FROM employees);
-- Returns NULL (correct, there is no second)
```

### Why does the interviewer care about reasoning?

These problems are proxies for daily SDET work: decomposition, data structures, complexity, edge cases.

```text
  Strong answer:
  "I use a LinkedHashMap for two reasons: first, it gives O(1)
   lookups for the count, and second, it preserves insertion order
   so I can find the FIRST non-repeating character. The time
   complexity is O(n) for two passes. Edge cases: empty string
   returns null, all-repeating returns null."

  Weak answer:
  "I loop through and check each character." (no complexity,
   no data structure justification, no edge cases)
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "What if the string is very long?" The two-pass approach is still O(n), which is optimal. You cannot do better.
- "Can you do the SQL one without window functions?" Yes, use the MAX subquery approach. It works on older databases.
- "Which duplicate do you keep?" The first encountered, because we use a seen-set that rejects subsequent duplicates.
